.he "icheck (1)                 8/16/26                   icheck (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
icheck
- check the block allocation of a file system
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
icheck [ -sv ] [ -b block ... ] filesystem
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Icheck reads every inode of the named file system and accounts for the
blocks it uses, checking each against the free list and against the
blocks already accounted for.  A block that appears twice is a
duplicate, and a block outside the file system is out of range; both
are reported.  It finishes with a count of the special, regular and
directory files and of the used and free blocks.
.sp
A check that only checks opens the file system read-only.  It is only
with -s, which rebuilds the free list, that anything is written.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-s	Rebuild the free list from the blocks the inodes say are in use.
The file system is opened for writing.

.ti -10
-v	Be verbose.

.ti -10
-b block	Report the inode and class of each use of block.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The free list is scanned before the inodes, so a block that is both in
the free list and in a file comes out as a duplicate.
.sp
Icheck is a read-only pass over the blocks, unlike fsck; it finds
duplicates and bad blocks but does not rewrite the inodes.  What it
finds, -s puts right.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
dcheck (1), ncheck (1)
